<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · c. 843 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Hymn of Kassiani</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="grc">Τροπάριο της Κασσιανής</bdi> / <bdi lang="grc-Latn">Tropario tis Kassianis</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A slow, sad hymn for Holy Week by Kassia, a nun of Constantinople in the 800s, the only woman whose music the Byzantine Church sang.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Kassia (one line attributed by legend to the Emperor Theophilos)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Kassia</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Byzantine Christians; the Eastern Orthodox Church</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Byzantine Greek</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Constantinople (her convent in the west of the city)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>9th century (Kassia lived c. 810 – c. 865; her convent was founded by 843)</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words and music by Kassia (9th century; she died around 865))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hymn-of-kassiani">Hymn of Kassiani</a>, also called the Troparion of Kassiani, is the most famous work of Kassia, a Byzantine composer, hymnographer and poet, and the only known woman whose music appears in the Byzantine liturgy. She was born between 805 and 810 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> into a wealthy family. Three Byzantine chroniclers, Pseudo-Symeon the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/logothete">Logothete</a>, George the Monk and Leo the Grammarian, tell that she took part in a &quot;bride show&quot; held by the Empress Dowager Euphrosyne for the young emperor Theophilos. Struck by her beauty, he remarked that through a woman had come the baser things, meaning Eve's sin; she answered that through a woman had come the better things, meaning the Incarnation through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a>. He rejected her and chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodora-wife-of-theophilos">Theodora</a>. By 843 Kassia had founded a convent in the west of Constantinople, near the Constantinian Walls, and become its first abbess; a letter of Theodore the Studite shows that she had religious reasons for the monastic life. Her convent was close to the nearby monastery of Stoudios, which re-edited the Byzantine liturgical books in the 9th and 10th centuries and so helped to preserve her work.</p><p>Kassia defended the veneration of icons against the iconoclast emperor Theophilos and was scourged for it, but remained outspoken: &quot;I hate silence when it is time to speak.&quot; The persecution ended after his death in 842, under Michael III and the regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodora-wife-of-theophilos">Theodora</a>. Kassia later travelled briefly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and settled on the island of Kasos, where she died around 865. She wrote both her poems and the music for them; about fifty of her hymns survive, most of them stichera, though the authorship of at least 26 is uncertain, and as with Romanos the Melodist and Andrew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> the earliest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> date from centuries after her life.</p><p>The hymn is chanted every year for Great and Holy Wednesday, as the doxastichon at the end of the aposticha of orthros and of the lamp-lighting psalms of the Presanctified Liturgy. A tradition tells how it was finished. In his later years Theophilos, still in love with her, rode to her monastery to see her once more; Kassia, now bound by her vow, hid in a closet and left the unfinished hymn on her table. The emperor found the papers, read them, wept, and added a line of his own, about Eve hiding in fear in Paradise at the sound of footsteps at dusk; after he had gone, Kassia read what he had written and finished the hymn. Many such stories, invented, grew up around the two of them.</p><p>The music is slow, sorrowful and plaintive and lasts some ten to twenty minutes, depending on tempo and style. It needs a wide vocal range and is counted among the most demanding works of solo Byzantine chant; choirs also sing it in unison, often over a Byzantine vocal bass drone. It has been recorded by Deborah Kayser and Nick Tsiavos (The Fallen Woman, 2008), in choral settings by members of Cappella Romana and the English Chamber Choir (2011), and by Cappella Romana under Alexander Lingas (2021). The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a> keeps Saint Kassiani's feast on 7 September, and in 2022 the Episcopal Church added her to its calendar on the same day.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A hymn for the Wednesday of Holy Week, sung slowly and mournfully. Among its images is Eve in Paradise, hearing footsteps at dusk and hiding in fear, the line the legend gives to the emperor.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Byzantine Greek</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Byzantine Greek</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">Κύριε, ἡ ἐν πολλαῖς ἁμαρτίαις περιπεσοῦσα γυνή,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="grc-Latn">Kyrie, hē en pollais hamartiais peripesousa gynē,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Lord, the woman who had fallen into many sins,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">τὴν σὴν αἰσθομένη θεότητα, μυροφόρου ἀναλαβοῦσα τάξιν,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="grc-Latn">tēn sēn aisthomenē theotēta, myrophorou analabousa taxin,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>perceiving your divinity, taking up the part of a myrrh-bearer,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">ὀδυρομένη, μύρα σοι, πρὸ τοῦ ἐνταφιασμοῦ κομίζει.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="grc-Latn">odyromenē, myra soi, pro tou entaphiasmou komizei.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>lamenting, brings you myrrh before your burial.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">Οἴμοι! λέγουσα, ὅτι νύξ μοι ὑπάρχει, οἶστρος ἀκολασίας,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="grc-Latn">Oimoi! legousa, hoti nyx moi hyparchei, oistros akolasias,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>&quot;Alas!&quot; she says, &quot;for night is upon me, a frenzy of licentiousness,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">ζοφώδης τε καὶ ἀσέληνος ἔρως τῆς ἁμαρτίας.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="grc-Latn">zophōdēs te kai aselēnos erōs tēs hamartias.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>dark and moonless, the love of sin.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">Δέξαι μου τὰς πηγὰς τῶν δακρύων,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="grc-Latn">Dexai mou tas pēgas tōn dakryōn,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Receive the springs of my tears,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">ὁ νεφέλαις διεξάγων τῆς θαλάσσης τὸ ὕδωρ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="grc-Latn">ho nephelais diexagōn tēs thalassēs to hydōr</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>you who by the clouds draw out the water of the sea;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">κάμφθητί μοι πρὸς τοὺς στεναγμοὺς τῆς καρδίας,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="grc-Latn">kamphthēti moi pros tous stenagmous tēs kardias,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>bend to me, to the groanings of my heart,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">ὁ κλίνας τοὺς οὐρανοὺς τῇ ἀφάτῳ σου κενώσει.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="grc-Latn">ho klinas tous ouranous tēi aphatōi sou kenōsei.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>you who bowed the heavens by your ineffable self-emptying.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">Καταφιλήσω τοὺς ἀχράντους σου πόδας,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="grc-Latn">Kataphilēsō tous achrantous sou podas,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I will kiss your spotless feet,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">ἀποσμήξω τούτους δὲ πάλιν τοῖς τῆς κεφαλῆς μου βοστρύχοις</span><span class="sg-r" lang="grc-Latn">aposmēxō toutous de palin tois tēs kephalēs mou bostrychois</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and wipe them again with the locks of my head,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">ὧν ἐν τῷ παραδείσῳ Εὔα τὸ δειλινόν,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="grc-Latn">hōn en tōi paradeisōi Eua to deilinon,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>whose footfall Eve in paradise, at evening,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">κρότον τοῖς ὠσὶν ἠχηθεῖσα, τῷ φόβῳ ἐκρύβη.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="grc-Latn">kroton tois ōsin ēchētheisa, tōi phobōi ekrybē.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>having heard it sound in her ears, hid herself in fear.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">Ἁμαρτιῶν μου τὰ πλήθη καὶ κριμάτων σου ἀβύσσους</span><span class="sg-r" lang="grc-Latn">Hamartiōn mou ta plēthē kai krimatōn sou abyssous</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The multitudes of my sins and the abysses of your judgements,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">τίς ἐξιχνιάσει, ψυχοσῶστα Σωτήρ μου;</span><span class="sg-r" lang="grc-Latn">tis exichniasei, psychosōsta Sōtēr mou;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>who will search them out, saviour of souls, my Saviour?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="grc">Μή με τὴν σὴν δούλην παρίδῃς, ὁ ἀμέτρητον ἔχων τὸ ἔλεος.</span><span class="sg-r" lang="grc-Latn">Mē me tēn sēn doulēn paridēis, ho ametrēton echōn to eleos.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Do not disregard me, your handmaid, you who have mercy without measure.&quot;</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Kassia (9th century), Greek text (Greek Wikisource). Text from <a href="https://el.wikisource.org/wiki/%CE%A4%CF%81%CE%BF%CF%80%CE%AC%CF%81%CE%B9%CE%BF_%CF%84%CE%B7%CF%82_%CE%9A%CE%B1%CF%83%CF%83%CE%B9%CE%B1%CE%BD%CE%AE%CF%82" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (classical transliteration (accents omitted; rough breathing as h; iota subscript written as i)).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (9)</summary><ul><li>Line 1.2: 'myrrh-bearer' (myrophoros) is the name given to the women who brought myrrh to anoint the body of Jesus.</li><li>Line 1.4: oistros akolasias, literally 'a gadfly (goad, frenzy) of licentiousness'.</li><li>Line 1.5: erōs tēs hamartias can be read as 'love of sin' or 'desire for sin'.</li><li>Line 1.7: diexagōn, 'drawing out' or 'leading through': the water of the sea is drawn up by the clouds.</li><li>Line 1.9: kenōsis, 'self-emptying', the theological term for God taking on human nature.</li><li>Lines 1.12-1.13: 'whose' (hōn) refers to the feet: Eve heard their footfall (kroton) in paradise in the evening and hid (Genesis 3:8). The Greek is one relative clause running over both lines.</li><li>Line 1.13: ēchētheisa, literally 'having been made to ring' in her ears with the sound.</li><li>Line 1.16: doulē, a female servant or slave; 'handmaid' in the biblical sense.</li><li>Line 1.15: the romanization keeps the Greek question mark ';' as written.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
